  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and storms are expected through this \nevening. A few storms could be strong to marginally severe. The main \nimpacts with any stronger storms will be gusty winds to 40 mph and \nan isolated flooding potential.\n\nThe bulk of this discussion will focus on the Tuesday through \nWednesday timeframe. There is a high level of uncertainty regarding \nthe timing and potential impacts during this timeframe as there are \nvarious model solutions. Generally though, we are looking at the \npossibility of several rounds of showers and storms with the \npotential of strong to severe storms from Tuesday morning through \nWendesday morning.\n\n\nTuesday morning: \n\nWe have an upper level trough and advancing cold front coming in \nfrom the Ohio River Valley. To our southeast we have high pressure \nout in the Atlantic and to our south a tropical disturbance along \nthe Gulf Coast. Most models keep us dry during the early portion of \nthe day but the 06Z and 12Z run of the HRRR are insistent on firing \nconvection across northeast TN and southwest VA Tuesday morning. If \nconvection fires, it will do so within an environment that is \nsupportive of a few strong to severe storms. The main threats will \nbe isolated damaging wind gusts and flooding. The probability of \nthis scenario is low as most models do not show this.\n\n\nTuesday afternoon/late evening:\n\n\nModels generally agree that a semi-organized to broken line of \nconvection will be approaching our northern areas (northern \nCumberland Plateau, northeast TN, southwest VA) from out of KY. This \ncould be in the form of one or multiple lines. Ahead of this line, \nwe may also see a few isolated storms pop up across the higher \nelevations of the east TN mountains, which a few of could be strong \nto severe. The big question for the activity out of KY is far south \ndoes it progress before it falls apart and loses its punch. Some \nmodels have the line greatly diminishing and some show a more \norganized and stronger line of convection moving down into our area. \nThe best case scenario during this timeframe is that convection is \nweakening as it moves into our area and we only see a few strong \nstorms with a low end threat for some isolated damaging winds and \nflooding. Worst case scenario, the line holds together and strong to \nsevere storms affect a good portion of the area with several reports \nof damaging winds, flooding, and an isolated tornado. \n\n\nLate Tuesday night/Wednesday morning:\n\nThe weather during this timeframe largely depends on how the earlier \nconvection evolves and moves through the area. There is a concerning \ntrend that I want to discuss though. The RRFS and NAM both generally \nshow the afternoon/early evening convection mostly dissipating \nbefore it enters our area. Both of these models then show a new line \nof convection firing along the remnant outflow boundary from the \nearlier storms. This new line of convection then slowly sags south \ninto our area during the overnight hours and remains into Wednesday \nmorning, possibly becoming stationary. This scenario would likely be \ndue to a slower frontal progression through the area, which would be \ndue to high pressure in the atlantic and the tropical depression to \nour south (no where for the front to go). This would be a worst case \nscenario as it would lead to the training of showers and storms and \nheavy rainfall over the course of several hours, which would likely \nlead to some significant flooding. Again, this is worst case \nscenario. The more likely scenario is that a line of showers and \nstorms have enough push behind them, from the front, that they move \nsouth at a decent progression. Even with this scenario though, \nisolated flooding is still possible due to recent rain and flooding \nacross the area. \n\n\nBottom line, this is a highly uncertain forecast in terms of system \nevolution and the resultant hazards and their severity. Please be \nprepared for severe weather anytime between Tuesday and Wednesday \nmorning and don't get caught off guard. \n\n\nFollowing the mid-week cold front, Thursday will be very nice with \nlow to mid 80s highs and dew points in the 60s under high pressure. \nTemperatures rise a couple of degrees into the weekend, but for the \nmost part remain below normal. A shortwave cycling the top of the \nupper ridge Friday into Saturday, may bring precipitation chances \nback to the area and unfortunately increased dew points yet again. A \npossible boundary that could get hung up to our south may complicate \nthings.",
